
do $chunk$
declare
  sec uuid;
  pass_id uuid;
  analysis_id uuid;
  next_ver int;
begin
  for sec in
    select distinct q.section_id
    from public.admin_questions q
    where q.source_group_id = 'RC-Z035'
      and q.section_id is not null
  loop
    select p.id into pass_id
    from public.admin_passages p
    where p.section_id = sec
      and p.source_group_id = 'RC-Z035'
    limit 1;

    if pass_id is null then
      insert into public.admin_passages (section_id, source_group_id, content)
      values (sec, 'RC-Z035', '<p style=''text-indent: 1em;''>In recent years, scholars have begun to use social science tools to analyze court opinions. These scholars have justifiably criticized traditional legal research for its focus on a few cases that may not be representative and its fascination with arcane matters that do not affect real people with real legal problems. Zirkel and Schoenfeld, for example, have championed the application of social science tools to the analysis of case law surrounding discrimination against women in higher education employment. Their studies have demonstrated how these social science tools may be used to serve the interests of scholars, lawyers, and prospective plaintiffs as well. However, their enthusiasm for the "outcomes analysis" technique seems misguided.</p><p style=''text-indent: 1em;''>Of fundamental concern is the outcomes analysts'' assumption that simply counting the number of successful and unsuccessful plaintiffs will be useful to prospective plaintiffs. Although the odds are clearly against the plaintiff in sex discrimination cases, plaintiffs who believe that their cause is just and that they will prevail are not swayed by such evidence. In addition, because lawsuits are so different in the details of the case, in the quality of the evidence the plaintiff presents, and in the attitude of the judge toward academic plaintiffs, giving prospective plaintiffs statistics about overall outcomes without analyzing the reason for these outcomes is of marginal assistance. Outcomes analysis, for example, ignores the fact that in certain academic sex discrimination cases—those involving serious procedural violations or incriminating evidence in the form of written admissions of discriminatory practices—plaintiffs are much more likely to prevail.</p><p style=''text-indent: 1em;''>Two different approaches offer more useful applications of social science tools in analyzing sex discrimination cases. One is a process called "policy capturing," in which the researcher reads each opinion; identifies variables discussed in the opinion, such as the regularity of employer evaluations of the plaintiff''s performance, training of evaluators, and the kind of evaluation instrument used; and then uses multivariate analysis to determine whether these variables predict the outcome of the lawsuit. The advantage of policy-capturing research is that it attempts to explain the reason for the outcome, rather than simply reporting the outcome, and identifies factors that contribute to a plaintiff''s success or failure. <lr data-itemid=''XD000748''>Taking a slightly different approach, other scholars have adopted a technique that requires reading complete transcripts of all sex discrimination cases litigated during a certain time period to identify variables such as the nature of the allegedly illegal conduct, the consequences for employers, and the nature of the remedy, as well as the factors that contributed to the verdict and the kind of evidence necessary for the plaintiff to prevail. While the findings of these studies are limited to the period covered, they assist potential plaintiffs and defendants in assessing their cases.</lr></p>')
      returning id into pass_id;
    else
      update public.admin_passages
      set content = coalesce(nullif('<p style=''text-indent: 1em;''>In recent years, scholars have begun to use social science tools to analyze court opinions. These scholars have justifiably criticized traditional legal research for its focus on a few cases that may not be representative and its fascination with arcane matters that do not affect real people with real legal problems. Zirkel and Schoenfeld, for example, have championed the application of social science tools to the analysis of case law surrounding discrimination against women in higher education employment. Their studies have demonstrated how these social science tools may be used to serve the interests of scholars, lawyers, and prospective plaintiffs as well. However, their enthusiasm for the "outcomes analysis" technique seems misguided.</p><p style=''text-indent: 1em;''>Of fundamental concern is the outcomes analysts'' assumption that simply counting the number of successful and unsuccessful plaintiffs will be useful to prospective plaintiffs. Although the odds are clearly against the plaintiff in sex discrimination cases, plaintiffs who believe that their cause is just and that they will prevail are not swayed by such evidence. In addition, because lawsuits are so different in the details of the case, in the quality of the evidence the plaintiff presents, and in the attitude of the judge toward academic plaintiffs, giving prospective plaintiffs statistics about overall outcomes without analyzing the reason for these outcomes is of marginal assistance. Outcomes analysis, for example, ignores the fact that in certain academic sex discrimination cases—those involving serious procedural violations or incriminating evidence in the form of written admissions of discriminatory practices—plaintiffs are much more likely to prevail.</p><p style=''text-indent: 1em;''>Two different approaches offer more useful applications of social science tools in analyzing sex discrimination cases. One is a process called "policy capturing," in which the researcher reads each opinion; identifies variables discussed in the opinion, such as the regularity of employer evaluations of the plaintiff''s performance, training of evaluators, and the kind of evaluation instrument used; and then uses multivariate analysis to determine whether these variables predict the outcome of the lawsuit. The advantage of policy-capturing research is that it attempts to explain the reason for the outcome, rather than simply reporting the outcome, and identifies factors that contribute to a plaintiff''s success or failure. <lr data-itemid=''XD000748''>Taking a slightly different approach, other scholars have adopted a technique that requires reading complete transcripts of all sex discrimination cases litigated during a certain time period to identify variables such as the nature of the allegedly illegal conduct, the consequences for employers, and the nature of the remedy, as well as the factors that contributed to the verdict and the kind of evidence necessary for the plaintiff to prevail. While the findings of these studies are limited to the period covered, they assist potential plaintiffs and defendants in assessing their cases.</lr></p>', ''), content),
          updated_at = now()
      where id = pass_id;
    end if;

    select coalesce(max(a.version), 0) + 1 into next_ver
    from public.admin_passage_analyses a
    where a.passage_id = pass_id;

    update public.admin_passage_analyses
    set status = 'draft', updated_at = now()
    where passage_id = pass_id and status = 'published';

    insert into public.admin_passage_analyses (passage_id, version, status, overall_html)
    values (pass_id, next_ver, 'published', nullif('<p>The passage endorses the broad project of applying social science tools to the analysis of sex discrimination case law while arguing that one particular technique, outcomes analysis, is of little practical use because counting wins and losses explains nothing about why cases are won or lost, and it recommends policy capturing and full-transcript analysis in its place. The author''s purpose is evaluative and corrective, moving from concession, to a targeted critique, to two better alternatives. The viewpoints to distinguish are the author''s and those of the social science scholars, Zirkel and Schoenfeld among them, whose general approach the author supports but whose enthusiasm for outcomes analysis the author rejects.</p>', ''))
    returning id into analysis_id;

    
    insert into public.admin_passage_analysis_segments (
      analysis_id, sort_order, part_label, segment_type, title, text_excerpt, explanation
    ) values (
      analysis_id, 1, 'P1', 'other', 'P1',
      'In recent years, scholars have begun to use social science tools to analyze court opinions. These scholars have justifiably criticized traditional legal research for its focus on a few cases that may not be representative and its fascination with arcane matters that do not affect real people with real legal problems. Zirkel and Schoenfeld, for example, have championed the application of social science tools to the analysis of case law surrounding discrimination against women in higher education employment. Their studies have demonstrated how these social science tools may be used to serve the interests of scholars, lawyers, and prospective plaintiffs as well. However, their enthusiasm for the "outcomes analysis" technique seems misguided.', '<p>The opening paragraph concedes before it attacks, and the shape matters. <strong>The social science scholars</strong> who analyse court opinions are, in the author''s view, right to criticize traditional legal research for fixating on a handful of possibly unrepresentative cases and on arcane matters that touch no real litigant. <strong>Zirkel and Schoenfeld</strong> are named as leaders in applying these tools to case law on sex discrimination in higher education employment, and the author grants that their work serves scholars, lawyers and prospective plaintiffs alike. The last sentence is the pivot the passage turns on: their enthusiasm for the ''outcomes analysis'' technique specifically seems misguided.</p>'
    );

    insert into public.admin_passage_analysis_segments (
      analysis_id, sort_order, part_label, segment_type, title, text_excerpt, explanation
    ) values (
      analysis_id, 2, 'P2', 'other', 'P2',
      'Of fundamental concern is the outcomes analysts'' assumption that simply counting the number of successful and unsuccessful plaintiffs will be useful to prospective plaintiffs. Although the odds are clearly against the plaintiff in sex discrimination cases, plaintiffs who believe that their cause is just and that they will prevail are not swayed by such evidence. In addition, because lawsuits are so different in the details of the case, in the quality of the evidence the plaintiff presents, and in the attitude of the judge toward academic plaintiffs, giving prospective plaintiffs statistics about overall outcomes without analyzing the reason for these outcomes is of marginal assistance. Outcomes analysis, for example, ignores the fact that in certain academic sex discrimination cases—those involving serious procedural violations or incriminating evidence in the form of written admissions of discriminatory practices—plaintiffs are much more likely to prevail.', '<p>Here the author makes the case against outcomes analysis, and the objections stack. The fundamental problem is the assumption that counting wins and losses helps prospective plaintiffs — but plaintiffs who believe their cause is just are not deterred by bad odds. Beyond that, cases differ so much in their facts, in the quality of evidence, and in a judge''s attitude toward academic plaintiffs that bare overall statistics, unaccompanied by any account of why cases came out as they did, offer only marginal help. The example seals it: outcomes analysis cannot see that plaintiffs alleging serious procedural violations or holding written admissions of discrimination are much more likely to win.</p>'
    );

    insert into public.admin_passage_analysis_segments (
      analysis_id, sort_order, part_label, segment_type, title, text_excerpt, explanation
    ) values (
      analysis_id, 3, 'P3', 'other', 'P3',
      'Two different approaches offer more useful applications of social science tools in analyzing sex discrimination cases. One is a process called "policy capturing," in which the researcher reads each opinion; identifies variables discussed in the opinion, such as the regularity of employer evaluations of the plaintiff''s performance, training of evaluators, and the kind of evaluation instrument used; and then uses multivariate analysis to determine whether these variables predict the outcome of the lawsuit. The advantage of policy-capturing research is that it attempts to explain the reason for the outcome, rather than simply reporting the outcome, and identifies factors that contribute to a plaintiff''s success or failure. Taking a slightly different approach, other scholars have adopted a technique that requires reading complete transcripts of all sex discrimination cases litigated during a certain time period to identify variables such as the nature of the allegedly illegal conduct, the consequences for employers, and the nature of the remedy, as well as the factors that contributed to the verdict and the kind of evidence necessary for the plaintiff to prevail. While the findings of these studies are limited to the period covered, they assist potential plaintiffs and defendants in assessing their cases.', '<p>The closing paragraph is constructive, offering two better uses of the same social science tools. In ''policy capturing'' a researcher reads each opinion, identifies variables such as how regularly the employer evaluated the plaintiff, how evaluators were trained and what instrument was used, then runs multivariate analysis to see which variables predict outcomes — the advantage being that it explains results rather than merely reporting them. The second approach reads complete transcripts from a set period to identify variables such as the conduct alleged, consequences for employers and the nature of the remedy, along with what actually drove the verdict. The author grants the limitation, findings bounded by the period studied, and still judges both useful to plaintiffs and defendants sizing up a case.</p>'
    );
  end loop;
end;
$chunk$;
